\chapter{Screen: the HDMI Console}
\label{ch:video}

Until now the system had one console: the serial line. On the Raspberry Pi 4B the second one is the HDMI port, and this chapter drives it.
There is no VGA text memory here: the graphics hardware shows a \emph{frame buffer}, an array of pixels in RAM, and the kernel has to draw every letter itself.
The chapter has four parts:

\begin{itemize}
\item the \textbf{mailbox} ([[mbox.c]]): the Raspberry Pi's graphics processor (the VideoCore) owns the display and the clocks. The ARM cores talk to it through
  a small hardware mailbox: a message is a list of ``tags'', each a question or a request (``how big is the screen?'', ``give me a buffer of 1920 by 1080 pixels'');
\item the \textbf{frame buffer} ([[fb.c]]): the message that asks for the buffer, and the pixel format in which we draw;
\item the \textbf{font}: bitmap fonts of the free \emph{Terminus} family (SIL Open Font License), converted at build time by [[tools/bdf2c.py]] from the BDF files in
  [[third_party/terminus]]. Three sizes (8$\times$16, 12$\times$24, 16$\times$32) are built in; the kernel takes the largest one that gives a screen of at least 80 columns and chooses by the
  width of the screen (so that a full HD or 4K monitor shows readable text and not a postage stamp);
\item the \textbf{terminal} ([[fbcon.c]]): the text on the screen is what the programs send to their terminal. They do not know that they talk to a display: they send an
  \emph{ANSI escape sequence} such as ``ESC [ 12 ; 40 H'' (move the cursor to line 12, column 40) or ``ESC [ 31 m'' (red text) like to any terminal emulator. [[fbcon.c]] is such an
  emulator, a small one: it understands what the programs of Ulix (the shell, nano, ncurses) need. It is fed with every byte that goes to the serial console
  ([[putch]]), so HDMI and serial line show the same.
\end{itemize}

The number of lines and columns is no longer fixed to 24 and 80: it follows from the resolution of the screen and the size of the font, and [[ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ)]] tells the programs.
(Command line of the kernel: [[video=1280x720]] asks for another resolution, [[video=off]] switches the screen off, [[fbfont=16]], [[fbfont=24]] or [[fbfont=32]]
fixes the font height.)

@
\section{The mailbox of the VideoCore (see \texttt{mbox.c})}
\label{src:mbox.c}

The mailbox has a write register, a read register and a status register. A message is written as one word: the \emph{bus address} of a buffer in RAM, in which the
tags are, plus the number of the channel (8: property tags, ``ARM to VideoCore'') in the lowest four bits (the buffer is aligned to 16 bytes, so these bits are free). The
VideoCore sees the RAM of the ARM cores at the bus address [[0xC0000000]] plus the physical address. It reads and writes the buffer itself, bypassing the caches of the ARM cores: before we send,
we write our cache lines back ([[cache_clean]]), and after the answer we throw away what we have cached ([[cache_flush_inval]]).

The buffer is a list: its size in bytes, the request code 0, the tags (each: tag number, size of the value buffer, request/response size, the values) and a tag 0 at the end. When
the VideoCore answers, the code is [[0x80000000]]. Qemu's [[raspi4b]] machine has the same mailbox, so this works without a board.

<<mbox.c>>=
#include "ulix.h"
#include "plat.h"
#include "cache.h"
#include "fb.h"

#define MB_READ     (*(volatile u32 *)(KERNEL_VA + plat.mbox + 0x00))
#define MB_STATUS   (*(volatile u32 *)(KERNEL_VA + plat.mbox + 0x18))        // bit 31: write full, bit 30: read empty
#define MB_WRITE    (*(volatile u32 *)(KERNEL_VA + plat.mbox + 0x20))
#define MB_FULL     0x80000000u
#define MB_EMPTY    0x40000000u
#define MB_CH_PROP  8
#define BUS_ALIAS   0xC0000000UL

int mbox_property (u32 *buf) {                       // buf: 16-byte aligned, buf[0] = size of the message in bytes. 0: answered, -1: no answer
  if (!plat.mbox) return -1;
  buf[1] = 0;
  cache_clean (buf, buf[0]);
  u32 msg = (u32) ((PA (buf) | BUS_ALIAS) & ~0xfUL) | MB_CH_PROP;
  for (int t = 0; MB_STATUS & MB_FULL; t++) if (t > 1000000) return -1;
  MB_WRITE = msg;
  for (int t = 0; t < 2000000; t++) {               // wait for our answer (other channels would be ignored)
    if (MB_STATUS & MB_EMPTY) continue;
    if (MB_READ != msg) continue;
    cache_flush_inval (buf, buf[0]);
    return buf[1] == 0x80000000u ? 0 : -1;
  }
  return -1;
}
