\section{The frame buffer (see \texttt{fb.c} and \texttt{fb.h})}
\label{src:fb.c}

The tags that make a screen: [[0x00040003]] (get the size of the display; with an HDMI monitor that is the resolution that it announces itself, its \emph{native} one), [[0x00048003]] (set
the physical size: what the display shows), [[0x00048004]] (set the virtual size: the part of the buffer in the picture; the same), [[0x00048005]] (depth in bits per pixel: 32),
[[0x00048006]] (pixel order), [[0x00040001]] (allocate the buffer: answer is its bus address and size) and [[0x00040008]] (bytes per line, the \emph{pitch}: it can be larger than width$\times$4).
With pixel order 0 a pixel is a 32 bit word [[0x00RRGGBB]].

The buffer lies in memory that belongs to the graphics processor, inside the first GByte of the address space, which the kernel has mapped as normal memory already. The graphics hardware reads it
from RAM, not from the caches: so after drawing the console writes the changed lines back ([[fb_clean]]).

<<fb.h>>=
#ifndef FB_H
#define FB_H
#include "ulix.h"

struct font { int w, h, bpr, n; const unsigned short *cp; const unsigned char *bits; };    // bpr: bytes per row of a glyph, n glyphs for the code points cp[]
extern const struct font fb_fonts[];
extern const int fb_nfonts;

struct framebuffer { u8 *base; u32 pitch, w, h; u64 pa, size; };
extern struct framebuffer fb;

int  mbox_property (u32 *buf);
int  fb_init (int want_w, int want_h);                 // 0: there is a screen
void fb_clean (const void *p, u64 len);                // write the cache lines back: the display reads RAM
const char *plat_cmdline (void);                       // dt.c
int  fbcon_init (void);                                // 0: the screen shows the console
void fbcon_putch (char c);
extern int fbcon_active, fbcon_cols, fbcon_rows;
#endif
