\section{The terminal on the screen (see \texttt{fbcon.c})}
\label{src:fbcon.c}

The terminal keeps the text in a grid of \emph{cells}: a character (a Unicode code point below 0x2600) and its colours, as packed into a 32 bit word. That grid is the truth; the pixels are a copy made by [[draw_cell]]. A cell is drawn
by looking up its glyph in the font (a bitmap of one bit per pixel, row by row) and writing the pixel in the foreground or background colour. That is all of graphics we need. When the screen
scrolls, we move the pixels (and the cells) instead of drawing all cells again.

The cursor is a block, drawn by drawing its cell with swapped colours; it is taken away before and put back after every character.

